# Tutorial 11 — Subroutines and Flow
# [call]/[return], @include, @scenario, @label — structuring scripts that scale.

@scenario {{SCENARIO_PREFIX}}_tutorial_11_subroutines
@include {{CHARACTERS_INCLUDE}}
{{BG_DECL}}@label start
[mode dialogue standard]
[set ui.dialogueUi project]
[set ui.textBoxAsset none]
[set ui.continueIndicator spinning_arrow]
[if tutorial_still]
[set ui.reduceMotion true]
[else]
[set ui.reduceMotion false]
[endif]
[if !tutorial_initialized]
[set tutorial_count 0]
[flag tutorial_initialized]
[endif]
[set ui.characterHeightFactor 1.5]
[set ui.characterBaselineY 1.5]
[set character.lavender.imageEffect none]
{{BG_START}}
[textspeed 28]
[autodelay 1650]
[show lavender center happy]
[wait 200]
Lavender: [jump] is a one-way trip. {b}[call]{/b} is a round trip — it goes to a label and comes back.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: That difference is the foundation of reusable script structure. Let me show you.

@label lesson_menu
[show lavender far_left talking]
> [call] and [return] — reusable blocks -> section_call
> @include — shared definition files -> section_include
> @scenario and @label — file structure -> section_structure
> Organizing a multi-file project -> section_organizing
> Quick reference: reusable flow -> quick_reference
> Practice: live call/return demo -> practice_scene
> Back to Tutorial Hub -> return_hub

# ─── Quick Reference ─────────────────────────────────────────────

@label quick_reference
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Reuse common beats instead of copying them:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@include /definitions/characters.vns{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[call common_warning]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@label common_warning{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}narrator: The air turns cold.{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[return]{/color}
narrator: Use {color=#8888cc}[goto OtherArc:start]{/color} for cross-scenario travel.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Checklist: include shared declarations once, keep subroutines short, and make every [call] reach a [return].
> Start the lesson sections -> section_call
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── [call] and [return] ────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_call
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: {b}[call label]{/b} jumps to a label, runs it, and returns to the line after the call.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Lavender: Let me check something.{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[call check_inventory]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Lavender: Okay, I'm back. Nothing useful in there.{/color}
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: The called label must end with {b}[return]{/b}:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@label check_inventory{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}narrator: You rummage through your bag.{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[return]{/color}
Lavender: Without [return], execution falls through into whatever label comes next — almost never what you want.
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: {b}[call] vs [jump]{/b}:
narrator: {b}[jump]{/b} — one-way. Goes to a label, stays there.
narrator: {b}[call]{/b} — round-trip. Goes to a label, runs it, returns to the caller.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Use [call] for reusable blocks: NPC greetings, flashbacks, stat checks, recurring setups.
> Next: @include -> section_include
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── @include ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_include
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: {b}@include{/b} inserts the contents of another file at the top of your script:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@include scripts/definitions/characters.vns{/color}
narrator: This pulls in all @character, @charimg, and @background declarations from that file.
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: Without @include, you'd have to copy character declarations into every script file.
Lavender: With @include, you declare once and share everywhere. Change the display name in one file, it updates across the project.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Common include files:
narrator: {b}characters.vns{/b} — all @character and @charimg declarations.
narrator: {b}backgrounds.vns{/b} — shared @background declarations.
narrator: {b}variables_init.vns{/b} — [set] commands for default variable values.
> Next: file structure -> section_structure
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── @scenario and @label ───────────────────────────────────────

@label section_structure
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: Every VNS file has a structure:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@scenario ch1_morning{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@include scripts/definitions/characters.vns{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@label start{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[bg bedroom_morning]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Lavender: Good morning!{/color}
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: {b}@scenario{/b} — declares this file's unique ID. Other files use it in [goto]:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[goto ch1_morning:start]{/color}
narrator: That navigates to the "start" label inside the ch1_morning scenario file.
Lavender: {b}@label{/b} — named anchor points within the file. Every file should have at least a {b}start{/b} label.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Think of @scenario as the file's address. @label is the room number within that address.
> Next: project organization -> section_organizing
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Project Organization ───────────────────────────────────────

@label section_organizing
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: As your game grows, file organization becomes critical:
narrator: {b}One scenario per file.{/b} Don't pack three scenes into one file.
narrator: {b}Descriptive names:{/b} ch1_morning.vns, ch1_confrontation.vns, ch2_library.vns.
narrator: {b}Folder structure:{/b}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}scripts/chapter_01/{/color} — all scenes for chapter 1.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}scripts/chapter_02/{/color} — all scenes for chapter 2.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}scripts/definitions/{/color} — shared includes (characters, backgrounds).
narrator: {color=#8888cc}scripts/tutorial/{/color} — you're looking at it.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Label naming within a file:
narrator: {b}Good:{/b} morning_routine, rooftop_confrontation, ending_brave
narrator: {b}Bad:{/b} label1, scene_a, thing
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Goal: anyone can open a file and understand where it fits without reading every other file first.
> Practice: live demo -> practice_scene
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Practice ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label practice_scene
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Watch [call] and [return] work in real time. I'll call two subroutines and you'll see execution return here each time.
[wait 300]
Lavender: Calling the greeting subroutine now:
[call sub_greeting]
Lavender: I'm back from the greeting. Now calling the inventory check:
[call sub_check]
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Both calls returned here. The script continued exactly where it left off.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Here's what the subroutines look like in script:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@label sub_greeting{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[show lavender center happy]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Lavender: Hi! This is the greeting subroutine.{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[return]{/color}
narrator: The [return] sends execution back to the line after [call sub_greeting].
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: Three real-world uses:
narrator: {b}1. Recurring NPC greetings{/b} — [call shopkeeper_hello] at the start of every shop visit.
narrator: {b}2. Flashbacks{/b} — [call flashback_childhood] dips into a memory, then returns to present.
narrator: {b}3. Utility checks{/b} — [call update_stats] runs logic silently and returns.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Start with one [call] block. Once you see how clean it makes repeated content, the rest follows naturally.
> Replay the demo -> practice_scene
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

@label sub_greeting
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Hi! This is the greeting subroutine. It runs and then...
[return]

@label sub_check
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Checking inventory... all clear.
[return]

@label return_hub
[if !tutorial_done_11]
[flag tutorial_done_11]
[inc tutorial_count]
[endif]
[goto {{HUB_TARGET}}:topics_page_2]
